hermes v0.23.0 (+ the three hermes-vault-payment-* patches): vault fill
scripts are reachable from page JavaScript; card, password and code fills
must run where the page cannot touch them.

Symptom (security review of #136981, reproduced on V8 and SpiderMonkey):
every secret-bearing fill script is evaluated with Runtime.evaluate in
the page's (or the card frame's) own JavaScript world, in sloppy mode.
Its first call into a page-replaceable built-in hands the page the
calling function through `hook.caller`, whose source holds the values:
- #136981's frame write starts with `Array.from(location.ancestorOrigins)`;
  a script on the checkout (third party, XSS) navigates the processor
  frame to its own site between the vault's check and the write (secret
  resolution can take seconds), and the write runs in the attacker's
  document and leaks number, expiry, CVC and name before it refuses;
- upstream's own top-level template (`build_fill_js`) calls
  `JSON.stringify` on its refusal path: a page that navigated after the
  vault's check gets the password, card or one-time code the same way.
The frame-context read (`_FRAME_CONTEXT_JS`) also ran in the frame's
world, where page code can fake the ancestry the vault decides on.

Fix:
- CDPSupervisor.evaluate_isolated: Page.createIsolatedWorld in the target
  frame (the page's main frame, or the OOPIF's own session), a random
  world name per call (Chromium reuses a named world, and runs scripts
  another CDP client registered for that name), Runtime.evaluate with that
  contextId and no user gesture; no fallback to the main world, no
  exception text back. Every secret write and every frame read (context,
  inspection) runs there.
- Two-phase writes: a value-free script first checks, in the same world,
  the document's origin, (card frame) that the bound page is its only
  ancestor, and that the inspection's stamps are still there; the values
  go out only when it passes (a failed check is eval_failed, missing
  stamps fields_changed). The fill script itself re-checks first. A write
  without its check is refused.
- Strict mode in the fill template, the frame ancestry guard and the
  page-backend secret wrapper; no call of any kind before the origin and
  ancestry checks (location reads, a plain-object refusal), so even a
  script run in a hostile world gives a hook nothing.
- The consent prompt names the processor frame origin(s) that will get
  card fields; a processor frame that was not named (swapped in after
  consent) gets nothing (frames_changed).
- Redaction also covers the combined expiry (MM/YY, MM / YY, MM/YYYY)
  and Diners 4-6-4 and 19-digit 6-13 card layouts.
- BrowserPageBackend: a secret script must run isolated from page code.
- Square's card-frame hosts (web.squarecdn.com, sandbox.) cite Square's
  CSP docs, which name them the SDK's only script and frame origins.

Tests: tests/tools/test_vault_fill_hostile_page.py runs the scripts in a
node document whose every built-in is hooked and walks .caller (control:
#136981's guard leaks there), the navigation race on the fill path, and
the CDP calls of evaluate_isolated; existing fakes answer the new calls.

Upstream: to report on NousResearch/hermes-agent#136981 (frames) and as
its own issue for the top-level template. Drop when upstream isolates.

